---
// A board that checked something the format claims, and the table it reported.
//
// The page is the record of one run: a still of the part, what the pins said, and the cases the run does not cover. It
// quotes the run rather than repeating it, because the build has no board attached.
//
// The numbers are split on purpose. The agreement and the refusal are about the medium; the block count, the logic and
// the rate are the realisation's cost on the part. Neither is a measurement of the engine, and the page says so rather
// than letting a reader take a flip-flop count for an engine figure.
import type { Artifact } from '../data/artifacts';

interface Props {
  artifact: Artifact;
}

const { artifact } = Astro.props;
---

<p class="run__lede">{artifact.summary}</p>

<figure class="run__figure device__figure">
  <img src="/board-ecp5.jpg" alt="The iCESugar-Pro v1.3 module seated in its carrier board, lit and connected." />
  <figcaption>
    The part the run was made on: an ECP5 LFE5U-25F on an iCESugar-Pro v1.3 module. The on-board iCELink carries three
    ECP5 balls to the host as GPIOs, so the verdict is read over the programming cable and no external wiring is
    involved. The carrier's two USB-C ports reach no FPGA.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What was checked</h2>
  <p>
    A volume of 11,172 records was written by the engine. The first 512 of them were read back off the medium, which is
    10,240 coordinate values, and supplied to the part as a ROM. The part decoded each value and accumulated the three
    axes; the host computed the same sum by division while the die computes it with a reciprocal multiply.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>A property of the medium</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Coordinate values read off the medium</td>
        <td>10,240, from 512 records</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Host sum, by division</td>
        <td><code>00002BFC</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Die sum, by reciprocal multiply</td>
        <td><code>00002BFC</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Values outside the 11,172-coordinate alphabet</td>
        <td>0</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>One value changed to 11,172, the first value outside it</td>
        <td>Sum mismatch, one value refused</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The last row is what makes the pass worth reading. One value at index 5,000 was changed and both the sum and the
    refusal count responded, so a pass is a comparison that ran rather than an indicator that can only show one value.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What it establishes, and for whom</h2>
  <p>
    The addresses on the medium are coordinates, and a decoder that did not write them says so. This is a statement
    about the format the engine uses, not a measurement of the engine: nothing here reports what the engine costs, how
    large its working set is, or how many reads an operation takes. Those are the engine's own numbers and they belong
    to the measurements page.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no table in the path on either side. The host reads the keys off the medium, and the part recomputes each
    coordinate from its value. A hash table cannot make this statement, because the statement is that no table exists.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>The realisation's cost</h2>
  <p>
    These are the part's numbers, not the engine's, and they are here because a decoder that is not cheap to realise is
    not a decoder anyone would put beside a core.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>The realisation on this part</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Block RAM</td>
        <td>9 of 56 blocks, 16 percent</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Logic</td>
        <td>219 flip-flops, 448 combinational</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Timing at a 40 MHz constraint</td>
        <td>76.99 MHz</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The rate is what the tool closed on this image, not a claim about speed. Where the decode stops matching is measured
    by the separate image that sweeps the valid range, which is built at 100, 120, 125 and 140 MHz.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What the run does not cover</h2>
  <p>
    512 of the 11,172 records are checked, because a ROM holding all 223,440 values does not fit the part. The ROM is
    built by the flow from the host's values, so what is established is the fit between the medium and the circuit, not
    a decoder reading a live medium.
  </p>
  <pre class="sr-only">--- values: the coordinate values of 512 records ---
records 512, values 10240, invalid 0, checksum 00002BFC
--- place and route: the volume image at 40 MHz ---
bitstream: out/tagma_volume_verify_top_40mhz.bit
--- program and read the verdict ---
pin_match   = 1
pin_invalid = 0
PASS: the die reproduced 00002BFC over the volume's values</pre>
</section>

<dl class="run__meta">
  <div>
    <dt>Check</dt>
    <dd><code>{artifact.source}</code>, in the engine checkout</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Image</dt>
    <dd><code>DESIGN=volume</code>, syntagma hardware flow</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Recorded</dt>
    <dd>2026-11-13</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
